1
00:00:02,332 --> 00:00:04,251
[MAN 06A panting]

2
00:00:16,806 --> 00:00:18,265
[chuckles]

3
00:00:18,307 --> 00:00:22,144
[THOMAS MICHAELSON groans]

4
00:00:22,185 --> 00:00:24,521
You're mad!

5
00:00:24,563 --> 00:00:26,231
[ENDERS]
There really is no difference

6
00:00:26,273 --> 00:00:28,066
between you and a swine!

7
00:00:28,108 --> 00:00:32,571
Even your pleas before death
sound like a squealing animal's!

8
00:00:32,613 --> 00:00:35,950
[THOMAS MICHAELSON]
No! Don't!

9
00:00:35,991 --> 00:00:38,076
- -[ENDERS grunts]
- -[THOMAS MICHAELSON screams]

10
00:02:11,545 --> 00:02:14,924
What a fine evening
this has been.

11
00:02:14,965 --> 00:02:17,426
There really is a
different zest to killing

12
00:02:17,467 --> 00:02:18,969
using your own hands.

13
00:02:19,011 --> 00:02:21,847
A gun can be so
clinical and unfeeling.

14
00:02:21,889 --> 00:02:25,183
I'll have to implement it
into my hunting regime.

15
00:02:27,061 --> 00:02:28,311
[knocking]

16
00:02:28,353 --> 00:02:29,229
[WILLIAM]
Lord Enders?

17
00:02:29,271 --> 00:02:30,940
[gasps]

18
00:02:30,981 --> 00:02:33,609
[WILLIAM]
It's Moriarty. Forgive me.

19
00:02:33,650 --> 00:02:35,652
[ENDERS]
Not Moriarty!

20
00:02:35,694 --> 00:02:38,280
This is bad! What do I do?

21
00:02:38,321 --> 00:02:41,116
If he finds the carcass,
I'm finished!

22
00:02:41,157 --> 00:02:42,826
[WILLIAM]
I'm letting myself in.

23
00:02:42,868 --> 00:02:43,827
[gasps]

24
00:02:43,869 --> 00:02:45,996
[WILLIAM]
Lord Enders? Are you in here?

25
00:02:46,038 --> 00:02:47,831
[ENDERS]
Wait. I'm just-- Just a moment!

26
00:02:47,873 --> 00:02:49,792
Oh.

27
00:02:51,251 --> 00:02:53,420
[ENDERS gasps]

28
00:02:55,505 --> 00:02:59,843
[WILLIAM] Dear sir,
it seems a murder has occurred.

29
00:02:59,885 --> 00:03:01,678
Surely, not by your hand?

30
00:03:01,720 --> 00:03:02,888
[ENDERS]
No! This isn't how it looks!

31
00:03:02,930 --> 00:03:04,180
I can explain!

32
00:03:04,222 --> 00:03:06,809
Listen! He attacked me with
a knife just moments ago!

33
00:03:06,850 --> 00:03:08,894
He's the criminal here!
Understand?

34
00:03:08,936 --> 00:03:10,604
[WILLIAM]
Was the motivation burglary?

35
00:03:10,646 --> 00:03:13,482
[ENDERS]
Yes! Exactly! He's a thief!

36
00:03:13,523 --> 00:03:15,692
Who knows how long
this despicable vermin

37
00:03:15,734 --> 00:03:17,569
was lurking in my stateroom!

38
00:03:17,611 --> 00:03:19,905
[WILLIAM] He wasn't very
effective at his craft.

39
00:03:19,947 --> 00:03:23,075
The room hasn't been ransacked
and nothing appears missing.

40
00:03:23,117 --> 00:03:24,451
Moreover,

41
00:03:24,493 --> 00:03:27,830
the knife was thrust through
his heart without hesitation.

42
00:03:27,871 --> 00:03:30,540
Not only that.
From what I can tell,

43
00:03:30,582 --> 00:03:32,918
the blade's handle
is exquisitely made.

44
00:03:32,960 --> 00:03:34,461
It doesn't strike me
as the sort of weapon

45
00:03:34,503 --> 00:03:36,797
a commoner would
carry with them.

46
00:03:38,340 --> 00:03:40,258
[WILLIAM]
It must belong to you.

47
00:03:40,300 --> 00:03:44,096
[gasps] Are you doubting
my word, Moriarty?

48
00:03:44,138 --> 00:03:45,597
[WILLIAM]
No.

49
00:03:45,639 --> 00:03:47,724
After all, you are a gentleman.

50
00:03:47,766 --> 00:03:50,352
Why shouldn't I believe
what you've told me?

51
00:03:50,393 --> 00:03:51,645
And on top of that,

52
00:03:51,687 --> 00:03:54,940
why would someone in your
position commit such an act?

53
00:03:54,982 --> 00:03:58,234
Murdering a commoner without
any reason? It's laughable.

54
00:03:58,276 --> 00:04:00,278
Right! It's ludicrous!

55
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:02,990
To us, but the
circumstantial evidence

56
00:04:03,032 --> 00:04:04,783
might not convince
an outside party,

57
00:04:04,825 --> 00:04:07,953
such as law enforcement,
for example.

58
00:04:07,995 --> 00:04:12,166
Has anyone other than myself
seen what transpired here?

59
00:04:12,207 --> 00:04:13,709
No. Just you.

60
00:04:13,750 --> 00:04:16,045
Well, that simplifies matters.

61
00:04:16,086 --> 00:04:20,215
Might I suggest we simply
discard the body overboard?

62
00:04:20,256 --> 00:04:23,010
Fortunately, we are at
sea on a moonless night.

63
00:04:23,052 --> 00:04:25,929
You couldn't ask for a more
favourable set of circumstances.

64
00:04:25,971 --> 00:04:29,349
We can toss this rotten apple
without anyone seeing.

65
00:04:35,731 --> 00:04:36,606
[WILLIAM]
Are you ready?

66
00:04:36,648 --> 00:04:38,192
Yes.

67
00:04:38,233 --> 00:04:40,318
[BOTH]
Heave, ho!

68
00:04:40,736 --> 00:04:42,487
[WILLIAM grunts]

69
00:04:42,529 --> 00:04:43,321
Pardon me.

70
00:04:43,363 --> 00:04:47,242
You're fine. No harm.

71
00:04:47,283 --> 00:04:49,786
I suppose we should
be getting back.

72
00:04:49,828 --> 00:04:52,039
There's nothing for you
to be concerned about.

73
00:04:52,081 --> 00:04:53,582
No one noticed a thing.

74
00:04:53,623 --> 00:04:54,875
Yes, but...

75
00:04:54,917 --> 00:04:57,544
On the off chance someone
does realize he is missing,

76
00:04:57,586 --> 00:04:59,546
the man was obviously
an alcoholic.

77
00:04:59,588 --> 00:05:03,508
They'll assume he was inebriated
and fell over the side.

78
00:05:03,550 --> 00:05:08,013
On ocean voyages, passengers
go missing all the time.

79
00:05:10,515 --> 00:05:12,559
You've a point.

80
00:05:12,601 --> 00:05:15,062
Yes! The blame
would fall on him!

81
00:05:15,104 --> 00:05:17,814
It goes without saying,
but this entire affair is--

82
00:05:17,856 --> 00:05:20,609
[WILLIAM grunts]
Of course. Our little secret.

83
00:05:20,650 --> 00:05:23,820
I owe you for this, Moriarty!

84
00:05:37,251 --> 00:05:40,921
My, I can barely
contain my excitement!

85
00:05:40,963 --> 00:05:43,548
But the ballet performance
won't be until tonight.

86
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:45,259
Is it not a trifle early for you

87
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:48,220
to be positively beside
yourself with anticipation?

88
00:05:48,262 --> 00:05:49,596
[NOBLEMAN 06A]
Not at all.

89
00:05:49,638 --> 00:05:51,265
Lord Enders has
gone to the trouble of

90
00:05:51,306 --> 00:05:53,058
saving a choice seat for me.

91
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:55,393
He really is a true nobleman.

92
00:05:55,435 --> 00:05:58,438
My word. How generous of him.

93
00:05:58,480 --> 00:06:01,275
They must be the
best in the house.

94
00:06:06,196 --> 00:06:10,033
[inaudible]

95
00:06:16,748 --> 00:06:20,877
[AUDIENCE clapping]

96
00:06:22,337 --> 00:06:25,423
Bravissimo! Marvellous!

97
00:06:25,465 --> 00:06:27,801
They'll talk of this
for years to come!

98
00:06:27,843 --> 00:06:29,636
To think we saw it.

99
00:06:29,678 --> 00:06:31,387
[NOBLEMAN 06A]
A triumphant showing.

100
00:06:31,429 --> 00:06:33,765
And with the conclusion
of the first act,

101
00:06:33,807 --> 00:06:35,558
I'll be taking my leave.

102
00:06:35,600 --> 00:06:38,812
[ENDERS] So soon?
But the ballet hasn't ended yet.

103
00:06:38,854 --> 00:06:41,731
Well, in truth, I actually
have a seat saved for me

104
00:06:41,773 --> 00:06:43,317
on the front row
for the second act.

105
00:06:43,358 --> 00:06:45,027
You can't be serious.

106
00:06:45,068 --> 00:06:47,654
It's madness to sit
amongst all the commoners!

107
00:06:47,696 --> 00:06:49,781
Yes, while I'll be
a tad uncomfortable

108
00:06:49,823 --> 00:06:51,825
surrounded by the lower classes,

109
00:06:51,867 --> 00:06:54,161
it's worth being
closer to the stage.

110
00:06:54,203 --> 00:06:56,496
[ENDERS sighs]
Is that so?

111
00:06:56,538 --> 00:06:59,333
Well then, at least
be mindful down there.

112
00:06:59,374 --> 00:07:01,668
Between us, I had the
most unpleasant encounter

113
00:07:01,710 --> 00:07:03,545
with their kind last night.

114
00:07:03,586 --> 00:07:05,339
[NOBLEMAN 06A]
Totally inexcusable.

115
00:07:05,380 --> 00:07:08,258
I'll take your advice to
heart and remain vigilant.

116
00:07:08,300 --> 00:07:09,885
Thank you again.

117
00:07:14,597 --> 00:07:17,100
[ENDERS] And to think
I respected the man.

118
00:07:17,142 --> 00:07:18,518
[knocking]

119
00:07:18,560 --> 00:07:22,731
[WILLIAM] Lord Enders,
might I have a moment with you?

120
00:07:22,772 --> 00:07:26,651
[ENDERS laughs]
Moriarty!

121
00:07:26,693 --> 00:07:30,197
Uh, what's wrong?
Your face is positively grim.

122
00:07:30,239 --> 00:07:31,823
[WILLIAM] Actually,

123
00:07:31,865 --> 00:07:34,617
the matter warrants the
expression.

124
00:07:34,659 --> 00:07:38,121
I have to ask...
Regarding our burglar...

125
00:07:38,163 --> 00:07:40,749
Are you absolutely sure
you killed the man?

126
00:07:40,790 --> 00:07:42,792
[ENDERS]
What?!

127
00:07:42,834 --> 00:07:43,960
[WILLIAM]
I hope I'm mistaken.

128
00:07:44,002 --> 00:07:45,379
Just what are you saying?!

129
00:07:45,420 --> 00:07:49,925
How could he be alive after we
threw the damn body overboard?

130
00:07:49,966 --> 00:07:54,388
[WILLIAM] Yes. It sounds
like fantasy, but look...

131
00:07:54,429 --> 00:07:56,014
[ENDERS gasps]

132
00:07:56,056 --> 00:07:58,767
Down in the audience.
Towards the left.

133
00:07:58,808 --> 00:08:02,896
Quite close to
the front of the stage.

134
00:08:02,938 --> 00:08:05,357
[ENDERS] And what exactly
am I looking for?

135
00:08:05,399 --> 00:08:07,609
[WILLIAM]
A bit more to the side.

136
00:08:07,650 --> 00:08:09,485
On the fourth row.

137
00:08:09,527 --> 00:08:11,613
Three seats from the end.

138
00:08:13,115 --> 00:08:15,784
Do you see him now?

139
00:08:15,825 --> 00:08:18,870
[ENDERS gasps]
But that's--!

140
00:08:18,912 --> 00:08:21,331
The thief from
your room last evening...

141
00:08:21,373 --> 00:08:22,874
...very much alive.

142
00:08:22,916 --> 00:08:24,376
[ENDERS]
That's not possible!

143
00:08:24,418 --> 00:08:27,087
I killed him
with my own two hands!

144
00:08:27,129 --> 00:08:28,838
It's difficult for me
to believe, too.

145
00:08:28,880 --> 00:08:31,466
And yet, I've no choice
but to trust my eyes.

146
00:08:31,507 --> 00:08:33,385
I wanted to inform you
as soon as possible.

147
00:08:33,427 --> 00:08:37,389
[ENDERS gasps] I see.
Very good of you, Moriarty.

148
00:08:37,431 --> 00:08:41,268
Forgive me,
but I must take my leave!

149
00:08:41,310 --> 00:08:42,769
Our lead dashes off.

150
00:08:42,811 --> 00:08:45,855
Onto the second act.

151
00:08:45,897 --> 00:08:46,815
[ENDERS panting]

152
00:08:46,856 --> 00:08:47,983
[WOMAN 07A yelps]

153
00:08:48,024 --> 00:08:49,943
[ENDERS]
This is bad! Really bad!

154
00:08:49,985 --> 00:08:50,860
I killed him!

155
00:08:50,902 --> 00:08:53,196
So what in hell's
name is going on?!

156
00:08:53,238 --> 00:08:54,281
What if he survived

157
00:08:54,323 --> 00:08:56,450
and somehow managed
to swim aboard the ship?

158
00:08:56,491 --> 00:08:59,369
No! Get a grip!
I'm positive he was dead!

159
00:08:59,411 --> 00:09:01,663
I've killed enough people;
I ought to know!

160
00:09:01,704 --> 00:09:03,957
But I have to be sure!

161
00:09:03,999 --> 00:09:05,000
[ENDERS grunts]

162
00:09:05,041 --> 00:09:07,336
[ENDERS pants]

163
00:09:07,377 --> 00:09:08,837
[ENDERS]
Where'd he go?! You!

164
00:09:08,878 --> 00:09:10,964
What happened to the man
who was sitting there?!

165
00:09:11,006 --> 00:09:14,259
Tell me which direction he went.
I'll pay you handsomely!

166
00:09:14,301 --> 00:09:16,386
There's no need
to give me any money, sir.

167
00:09:16,428 --> 00:09:19,889
He got up and left through
that doorway just now.

168
00:09:19,931 --> 00:09:22,058
You're bloody welcome!

169
00:09:22,100 --> 00:09:25,103
[ENDERS] Damn! Damn! Damn!
Then he is alive!

170
00:09:25,145 --> 00:09:27,230
If he tells anyone
about what happened,

171
00:09:27,272 --> 00:09:28,606
how I tried to kill him,

172
00:09:28,648 --> 00:09:32,568
there'll be an investigation!
It could be ruinous for me!

173
00:09:38,158 --> 00:09:43,913
[music plays]

174
00:09:49,169 --> 00:09:50,295
[MORAN]
Director,

175
00:09:50,337 --> 00:09:52,881
our lead should be hitting
his mark as scheduled.

176
00:10:02,224 --> 00:10:04,184
- -[footsteps]
- -[ENDERS panting]

177
00:10:04,226 --> 00:10:08,563
[ENDERS] This way.
I'm certain he came down here.

178
00:10:08,604 --> 00:10:11,649
All that's left is to
find where he's hiding--

179
00:10:19,115 --> 00:10:22,327
[ENDERS] You've put me
through a great deal of trouble.

180
00:10:25,914 --> 00:10:28,958
[ENDERS gasps]
He is dead.

181
00:10:29,000 --> 00:10:30,544
Then how did he...?

182
00:10:30,585 --> 00:10:32,670
What's the meaning of this?!

183
00:10:32,712 --> 00:10:34,714
I saw you walking
in the hallway!

184
00:10:34,756 --> 00:10:37,842
You rotten scum!
You miserable--!

185
00:10:37,884 --> 00:10:41,388
I knew it! I snuffed
him out last night!

186
00:10:41,430 --> 00:10:44,724
So then how did he
come back to life

187
00:10:44,766 --> 00:10:47,810
only to wind up dead
all over again?!

188
00:10:47,852 --> 00:10:49,145
Wait.

189
00:10:49,187 --> 00:10:53,316
How do I know he won't suddenly
resurrect a second time?

190
00:10:53,358 --> 00:10:55,735
I won't make the same mistake.

191
00:10:55,777 --> 00:10:57,946
I'll have to kill him once more.

192
00:10:57,987 --> 00:11:01,991
And this time
be absolutely sure.

193
00:11:05,287 --> 00:11:07,830
He's ready. It's set.

194
00:11:07,872 --> 00:11:09,416
Waiting for your cue.

195
00:11:09,458 --> 00:11:11,126
Go ahead and raise him up.

196
00:11:11,167 --> 00:11:12,961
[MORAN]
Understood.

197
00:11:21,677 --> 00:11:24,556
[ENDERS]
Die! Die! Die!

198
00:11:24,598 --> 00:11:26,808
You persistent unwashed filth!

199
00:11:26,849 --> 00:11:29,936
Never will you again
come back to life!

200
00:11:29,978 --> 00:11:31,605
Stay dead this time!

201
00:11:31,646 --> 00:11:34,065
You rotten no good!

202
00:11:34,107 --> 00:11:36,901
[ENDERS grunting]

203
00:11:36,943 --> 00:11:38,653
[gasps]

204
00:11:54,002 --> 00:11:59,132
[screams] He just
killed that man!

205
00:11:59,174 --> 00:12:01,717
No, he was already
dead, though.

206
00:12:01,759 --> 00:12:05,305
And I was making sure...

207
00:12:05,347 --> 00:12:07,516
[AUDIENCE 07A] He stabbed
the pour soul in cold blood!

208
00:12:07,557 --> 00:12:09,601
[AUDIENCE 07B] I-It isn't part
of the show! This is real!

209
00:12:09,643 --> 00:12:10,893
[AUDIENCE 07A]
What a monster!

210
00:12:10,935 --> 00:12:13,313
[AUDIENCE 07B] He killed him
right there on the stage?

211
00:12:13,355 --> 00:12:18,360
Wait. Unless I'm mistaken,
is that not Lord Blitz Enders?

212
00:12:18,401 --> 00:12:19,361
[gasps]

213
00:12:19,402 --> 00:12:20,612
[AUDIENCE murmuring]

214
00:12:20,654 --> 00:12:21,988
[AUDIENCE 07C]
It can't be.

215
00:12:22,030 --> 00:12:23,865
[AUDIENCE 07D]
But he's an aristocrat.

216
00:12:23,906 --> 00:12:26,742
[AUDIENCE 07E]
How could he do such a thing?

217
00:12:26,784 --> 00:12:29,078
Then the stories
about him are true.

218
00:12:29,120 --> 00:12:32,541
He does hunt people,
and now he's doing it here!

219
00:12:32,582 --> 00:12:34,876
[NOBLEWOMAN 07B]
How awful! He's a murderer!

220
00:12:34,917 --> 00:12:37,920
[AUDIENCE 07F] What gives you
the right to take a human life?

221
00:12:37,962 --> 00:12:41,841
[AUDIENCE shouting]

222
00:12:46,262 --> 00:12:47,930
Human life, you say?

223
00:12:47,972 --> 00:12:51,643
Come now. Don't make me laugh.

224
00:12:51,685 --> 00:12:54,563
You think you've witnessed
the murder of a commoner?

225
00:12:54,604 --> 00:12:57,440
Fools! I can do as
I please with these beasts!

226
00:12:57,482 --> 00:12:59,942
[AUDIENCE gasps]

227
00:12:59,984 --> 00:13:02,487
This is Noah's Ark,
vessel for the chosen!

228
00:13:02,529 --> 00:13:06,115
If you're not of noble blood,
you're no better than livestock!

229
00:13:06,157 --> 00:13:07,783
And I have every right to decide

230
00:13:07,825 --> 00:13:10,745
whether you live or die
by my hands!

231
00:13:11,663 --> 00:13:14,416
[NOBLEMAN 06A grunts]
He's gone completely mad!

232
00:13:14,457 --> 00:13:17,335
No matter what you call it,
you've still committed murder!

233
00:13:17,377 --> 00:13:19,921
[gasps] You hypocritical cad!

234
00:13:19,962 --> 00:13:21,797
You've always look down
on the peasants,

235
00:13:21,839 --> 00:13:24,300
the same as me,
and don't pretend otherwise!

236
00:13:24,342 --> 00:13:26,386
Don't you dare
compare me to yourself!

237
00:13:26,428 --> 00:13:28,221
I'm not some heartless killer!

238
00:13:28,263 --> 00:13:30,973
[ENDERS grunts]
You traitor!

239
00:13:31,015 --> 00:13:35,770
[AUDIENCE yells]

240
00:13:35,811 --> 00:13:37,564
[ENDERS grunts]

241
00:13:37,606 --> 00:13:39,190
[ENDERS groans]

242
00:13:41,693 --> 00:13:43,945
[ENDERS groans]

243
00:13:43,986 --> 00:13:44,820
[SHIPMAN 07A]
Stop him!

244
00:13:44,862 --> 00:13:46,698
[SHIPMAN 07B]
Apprehend that man!

245
00:13:47,990 --> 00:13:49,242
[SHIPMAN 07A]
Don't let him escape!

246
00:13:49,284 --> 00:13:50,910
[SHIPMAN 07B]
Seize him!

247
00:14:06,884 --> 00:14:10,179
[HOLMES]
Hmm.

248
00:14:10,221 --> 00:14:13,391
Now that's interesting.

249
00:14:13,433 --> 00:14:18,146
[ENDERS panting]

250
00:14:32,952 --> 00:14:33,620
[ENDERS grunts]

251
00:14:33,662 --> 00:14:35,371
[SHIPMAN 07C]
Stop there!

252
00:14:36,748 --> 00:14:37,624
[SHIPMEN shout]

253
00:14:37,666 --> 00:14:40,794
[panting]

254
00:14:40,835 --> 00:14:46,215
[CROWD murmurs]

255
00:14:46,257 --> 00:14:49,051
[NOBLEMAN 06A grunts]
He's up there, on the mast!

256
00:14:50,637 --> 00:14:55,642
[ENDERS cackles]

257
00:14:55,684 --> 00:14:58,645
[ENDERS] Those beasts!
They can't reach me up here!

258
00:14:58,687 --> 00:15:00,563
For the rest of
their pointless lives,

259
00:15:00,605 --> 00:15:02,482
they'll be beneath me,
looking up!

260
00:15:02,524 --> 00:15:06,068
For I am the chosen among men,
a true successor of Noah!

261
00:15:06,110 --> 00:15:10,448
[cackling]

262
00:15:10,490 --> 00:15:12,534
[ENDERS yelps]

263
00:15:12,575 --> 00:15:14,828
[gasping]

264
00:15:23,961 --> 00:15:26,881
[gasps]

265
00:15:29,676 --> 00:15:36,098
[yells]

266
00:15:36,140 --> 00:15:39,060
[ENDERS]
Moriarty...

267
00:15:41,563 --> 00:15:43,022
[WILLIAM chuckles]

268
00:15:43,064 --> 00:15:44,774
[ENDERS gasps]

269
00:15:45,983 --> 00:15:48,986
[ENDERS screams]

270
00:16:10,675 --> 00:16:12,761
You're back. Would
you care for some tea?

271
00:16:12,802 --> 00:16:14,846
[MORAN]
Nah. Make it a whisky.

272
00:16:14,888 --> 00:16:17,306
[LOUIS] We have the
bottle ready for you.

273
00:16:19,183 --> 00:16:21,853
So what was the
sentiment around town?

274
00:16:21,895 --> 00:16:24,063
[MORAN]
Everyone's in an uproar.

275
00:16:24,105 --> 00:16:26,441
Death on the Noahtic's
maiden voyage?

276
00:16:26,482 --> 00:16:28,943
Naturally everyone's
talking about it.

277
00:16:28,984 --> 00:16:32,029
The cat's out of the bag and
there's no hiding it.

278
00:16:32,071 --> 00:16:34,783
It gets better. There's
a rumor going around

279
00:16:34,824 --> 00:16:37,660
that Enders hunted people
on his estate grounds.

280
00:16:37,702 --> 00:16:38,870
In response,

281
00:16:38,912 --> 00:16:41,372
some MPs in the House of
Commons are speaking out,

282
00:16:41,414 --> 00:16:44,834
demanding for a full
investigation of his properties.

283
00:16:44,876 --> 00:16:46,878
When a search is
finally carried out

284
00:16:46,920 --> 00:16:48,546
and the remains of
those who were hunted

285
00:16:48,588 --> 00:16:51,215
by the late Lord Enders
are eventually unearthed,

286
00:16:51,257 --> 00:16:55,470
I don't see how the country
could be anything but appalled.

287
00:16:55,511 --> 00:16:57,263
Your smile tells me
you must have

288
00:16:57,305 --> 00:16:59,849
already taken a few measures
into your own hands.

289
00:16:59,891 --> 00:17:01,893
[WILLIAM] My brother's
exercising some influence.

290
00:17:01,935 --> 00:17:03,978
It'll likely turn out
to be favorable.

291
00:17:04,019 --> 00:17:05,104
No getting past--

292
00:17:05,146 --> 00:17:07,231
that was a large-scale
operation we undertook.

293
00:17:07,273 --> 00:17:09,609
Can't expect the clean-up
to be any different.

294
00:17:09,651 --> 00:17:11,068
Right, Fred?

295
00:17:11,110 --> 00:17:13,070
Hey, Fred.

296
00:17:13,112 --> 00:17:16,741
Tch. Mister personality
over there.

297
00:17:16,783 --> 00:17:19,076
But, really,
despite its complexity,

298
00:17:19,118 --> 00:17:21,830
I'm surprised how
smoothly things went.

299
00:17:21,871 --> 00:17:23,372
You must have had
the entire painting

300
00:17:23,414 --> 00:17:25,583
visualized from the beginning.

301
00:17:25,625 --> 00:17:27,042
Yes,

302
00:17:27,084 --> 00:17:30,463
but it only went off without a
hitch because of those involved.

303
00:17:30,505 --> 00:17:32,423
What's more,

304
00:17:32,465 --> 00:17:36,010
one unresolved matter
causes me some trepidation.

305
00:17:38,053 --> 00:17:39,221
[WILLIAM]
While it's still too early

306
00:17:39,263 --> 00:17:41,599
to ascertain the
threat this man poses,

307
00:17:41,641 --> 00:17:45,269
no doubt I'll be forced to make
some inquiries concerning him.

308
00:17:45,311 --> 00:17:46,145
[MORAN] Give the word.

309
00:17:46,187 --> 00:17:47,897
If this guy ends up being our
foe,

310
00:17:47,939 --> 00:17:51,567
we need to know ahead of time
how to deal with him.

311
00:17:51,609 --> 00:17:52,694
I saw him again.

312
00:17:52,735 --> 00:17:55,697
He stopped me as we were
disembarking the ship.

313
00:17:55,738 --> 00:17:58,867
And we had the most
interesting conversation.

314
00:18:09,335 --> 00:18:11,212
[HOLMES]
Hey! Professor!

315
00:18:12,588 --> 00:18:14,674
I've been looking for you.

316
00:18:14,716 --> 00:18:15,591
I needed someone to talk through

317
00:18:15,633 --> 00:18:17,552
that unforgettable
performance with.

318
00:18:17,593 --> 00:18:20,221
Someone who could
keep pace with me.

319
00:18:20,262 --> 00:18:22,348
That was quite the
spectacle, wasn't it?

320
00:18:22,390 --> 00:18:23,683
But when it comes to ballet,

321
00:18:23,725 --> 00:18:24,767
I think you'll find

322
00:18:24,809 --> 00:18:25,560
there are far more
knowledgeable passengers--

323
00:18:25,601 --> 00:18:27,854
You're playing dumb, professor.

324
00:18:27,896 --> 00:18:31,399
I'm talking about the dramatic
murder in full view of everyone.

325
00:18:34,652 --> 00:18:37,154
That was no ordinary murder.

326
00:18:37,196 --> 00:18:39,657
Are you implying it
was somehow contrived?

327
00:18:39,699 --> 00:18:41,784
[HOLMES]
We'll start with the body.

328
00:18:41,826 --> 00:18:44,954
Our supporting character
died the night before.

329
00:18:44,996 --> 00:18:46,664
From his jaw to the limbs

330
00:18:46,706 --> 00:18:49,584
post-mortem rigidity
had already set in.

331
00:18:49,625 --> 00:18:53,796
Meaning he'd been dead
ten to fifteen hours already.

332
00:18:53,838 --> 00:18:55,130
Any way you slice it,

333
00:18:55,172 --> 00:18:57,926
there's no possible way he met
his end up on that stage.

334
00:18:57,967 --> 00:19:00,803
[WILLIAM] Your interests extend
to forensic pathology, do they?

335
00:19:00,845 --> 00:19:03,431
Picked it up in France
where it's common knowledge.

336
00:19:03,472 --> 00:19:06,642
This country is quite
behind the times.

337
00:19:06,684 --> 00:19:08,519
But we're getting off track.

338
00:19:08,561 --> 00:19:11,355
Back to the highly
irregular matter.

339
00:19:11,397 --> 00:19:13,148
As the horrified
audience watched,

340
00:19:13,190 --> 00:19:16,527
Lord Enders thrust a dagger over
and over into the victim's body.

341
00:19:16,569 --> 00:19:18,947
Supposedly this was when the man
died.

342
00:19:18,988 --> 00:19:22,075
Afterwards, the Count said
something very revealing.

343
00:19:22,116 --> 00:19:23,576
Human life, you say?

344
00:19:23,618 --> 00:19:26,829
Come now. Don't make me laugh.

345
00:19:26,871 --> 00:19:29,874
You think you've witnessed
the murder of a commoner?

346
00:19:29,916 --> 00:19:31,125
[HOLMES]
Meaning,

347
00:19:31,166 --> 00:19:34,503
he thought he had murdered
his victim while on stage.

348
00:19:34,545 --> 00:19:36,714
And yet the man had
clearly been dead

349
00:19:36,756 --> 00:19:39,050
since the previous evening.

350
00:19:39,092 --> 00:19:41,302
Odd, isn't it?

351
00:19:41,343 --> 00:19:43,596
Indeed. Quite odd.

352
00:19:43,638 --> 00:19:45,389
[HOLMES]
It follows, then that the Count

353
00:19:45,431 --> 00:19:47,182
was under the
grave misapprehension

354
00:19:47,224 --> 00:19:49,602
the corpse wasn't a corpse.

355
00:19:49,644 --> 00:19:51,186
As if he had been
led into thinking

356
00:19:51,228 --> 00:19:53,314
the man were still alive.

357
00:19:53,355 --> 00:19:55,441
Stands to reason

358
00:19:55,483 --> 00:19:59,528
someone was complicit in making
him believe this charade.

359
00:19:59,570 --> 00:20:01,363
As in a third party?

360
00:20:01,405 --> 00:20:04,199
[HOLMES] Indeed.
Despite how wild it sounds,

361
00:20:04,241 --> 00:20:06,661
it's the only explanation
that makes sense.

362
00:20:06,702 --> 00:20:08,412
My theory--

363
00:20:08,454 --> 00:20:11,165
this ship served as
the stage for the event.

364
00:20:11,206 --> 00:20:14,209
Through stratagems orchestrated
by persons unknown,

365
00:20:14,251 --> 00:20:16,504
the Count became the actor
in a performance,

366
00:20:16,545 --> 00:20:18,840
completely unaware of
the role he was playing

367
00:20:18,881 --> 00:20:20,925
or its importance.

368
00:20:20,967 --> 00:20:22,510
It sounds like
you're suggesting

369
00:20:22,551 --> 00:20:26,139
some elaborate conspiracy
transpired on the voyage.

370
00:20:26,180 --> 00:20:27,932
What was the end goal?

371
00:20:27,974 --> 00:20:31,185
That is precisely the
mystery I'd like to unravel.

372
00:20:31,226 --> 00:20:33,437
I'm merely stating if
such events took place,

373
00:20:33,479 --> 00:20:35,648
this fiasco would make sense.

374
00:20:35,690 --> 00:20:37,150
I did some digging
around the ship

375
00:20:37,191 --> 00:20:39,610
to see if I could find
some additional clues.

376
00:20:39,652 --> 00:20:43,156
But sadly, wasn't able to
uncover anything worth noting.

377
00:20:43,197 --> 00:20:45,783
That is an awful lot
of trouble to go through.

378
00:20:45,825 --> 00:20:47,743
Despite calling it
a mere theory,

379
00:20:47,785 --> 00:20:49,954
you seem quite sure of yourself.

380
00:20:49,996 --> 00:20:51,622
[HOLMES]
Of course, I am.

381
00:20:51,664 --> 00:20:54,792
After you eliminate
every other possibility,

382
00:20:54,834 --> 00:20:57,419
then whatever remains,
must be the truth

383
00:20:57,461 --> 00:20:59,881
no matter how improbable.

384
00:21:03,551 --> 00:21:06,470
It's odd. Seems like
you're enjoying this.

385
00:21:06,512 --> 00:21:10,099
Why wouldn't I be? Despite
not having the foggiest notion

386
00:21:10,141 --> 00:21:12,101
who did this or
their motivations,

387
00:21:12,143 --> 00:21:15,855
the culprit has provided me
with an exquisite mystery!

388
00:21:15,897 --> 00:21:17,606
You're a mathematician
after all.

389
00:21:17,648 --> 00:21:19,859
You can understand my delight.

390
00:21:19,901 --> 00:21:23,696
Like your equations,
the joy lies in the solution.

391
00:21:24,655 --> 00:21:28,784
Yes. Guilty.

392
00:21:28,826 --> 00:21:31,328
This has been
a stimulating conversation.

393
00:21:31,370 --> 00:21:34,040
But if you'll pardon me,
I must be leaving.

394
00:21:34,082 --> 00:21:36,417
[HOLMES]
Sure. Good day.

395
00:21:38,293 --> 00:21:40,379
[WILLIAM]
How rude of me.

396
00:21:40,421 --> 00:21:44,967
All this time, and I realized
I never asked you your name.

397
00:21:45,009 --> 00:21:47,970
You can call me Holmes,
old sport.

398
00:21:51,432 --> 00:21:54,268
Detective Sherlock Holmes.

399
00:23:33,534 --> 00:23:36,204
[FRED] Next time
on Moriarty the Patriot  .

400
00:23:36,245 --> 00:23:38,206
"A Study in 'S' Act One.'"
